# Blazor Standards

This project uses:

- .NET 9
- Blazor Server
- MudBlazor
- Clean Architecture

---

# Component Design

Prefer small reusable components.

Avoid components larger than 300 lines.

Move business logic out of Razor files.

Use partial classes (.razor.cs) for complex components.

Keep markup clean.

Avoid duplicated UI.

---

# State Management

Prefer local component state.

Use cascading parameters only when necessary.

Avoid static state.

Avoid singleton state unless explicitly required.

---

# Dependency Injection

Use @inject only for services.

Never resolve services manually.

Never use IServiceProvider.

Constructor injection is preferred in .razor.cs.

---

# Data Loading

Always use async/await.

Support CancellationToken whenever possible.

Avoid synchronous calls.

Use OnInitializedAsync.

Use OnParametersSetAsync only when parameters affect loading.

Never perform long-running work inside rendering.

---

# Rendering

Minimize unnecessary StateHasChanged() calls.

Do not call StateHasChanged() unless required.

Avoid expensive LINQ expressions inside markup.

Avoid creating objects inside Razor expressions.

---

# Parameters

Use [Parameter] for external inputs.

Use [EditorRequired] for mandatory parameters.

Validate parameter values.

Do not mutate parameter values directly.

---

# Events

Prefer EventCallback<T>.

Avoid Action delegates.

Avoid anonymous lambdas when reusable methods exist.

---

# Forms

Use MudForm.

Use FluentValidation.

Do not duplicate validation logic.

Show user-friendly validation messages.

---

# Tables

Prefer MudTable.

Use ServerData for large datasets.

Enable paging.

Enable sorting.

Enable filtering.

Avoid loading all records.

---

# Dialogs

Use MudDialog.

Return strongly typed results.

Avoid nested dialogs.

---

# Notifications

Use ISnackbar.

Use appropriate severity.

Do not show technical exception messages.

---

# Error Handling

Catch only expected exceptions.

Log unexpected exceptions.

Display friendly messages.

Never swallow exceptions silently.

---

# Styling

Prefer MudBlazor components.

Avoid inline styles.

Use CSS isolation when appropriate.

Reuse existing theme variables.

---

# Performance

Use @key where appropriate.

Use Virtualize for large lists.

Avoid unnecessary renders.

Do not allocate objects during rendering.

---

# JavaScript

Prefer Blazor APIs.

Use JavaScript only when Blazor cannot solve the problem.

Wrap JS Interop inside services.

Never call JS directly from multiple components.

---

# Navigation

Use NavigationManager.

Avoid hardcoded URLs.

Centralize route constants.

---

# File Structure

MyComponent.razor

MyComponent.razor.cs

MyComponent.razor.css

MyComponentTests.cs

---

# Output Expectations

Generated components should be:

- Clean
- Reusable
- Responsive
- Async
- Testable
- Accessible
- Production Ready

Always follow MudBlazor best practices.

Never generate quick-and-dirty components.